1
00:00:00,155 --> 00:00:01,705
<u>Async Processing & Messaging</u>

2
00:00:03,022 --> 00:00:04,522
<i>What is asynchronous processing?</i>

3
00:00:05,794 --> 00:00:08,474
Asynchronous processing separates acceptance from completion.

4
00:00:09,174 --> 00:00:13,214
The system records intent, returns a pending or accepted state, and performs the work later.

5
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:16,756
<i>Why move work out of the request-response path?</i>

6
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:21,771
Slow or expensive work can exceed request budgets and consume server resources.

7
00:00:22,481 --> 00:00:25,781
Moving it to background workers can improve responsiveness and absorb bursts.

8
00:00:27,313 --> 00:00:28,013
<i>What is a queue?</i>

9
00:00:29,534 --> 00:00:31,884
A queue buffers work between producers and consumers.

10
00:00:32,584 --> 00:00:36,824
It helps control concurrency and lets workers process jobs at a rate downstream systems can sustain.

11
00:00:38,345 --> 00:00:39,205
<i>What is a producer?</i>

12
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:43,725
A producer creates and submits a message or job to a queue or broker.

13
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:47,325
It should define the payload contract and what durable acceptance means.

14
00:00:48,849 --> 00:00:50,129
<i>What is a consumer or worker?</i>

15
00:00:51,651 --> 00:00:54,621
A consumer or worker receives queued work and performs the processing.

16
00:00:55,331 --> 00:00:59,601
It must handle retries, duplicates, failures, and observability according to the delivery model.

17
00:01:01,105 --> 00:01:02,265
<i>What is pub/sub?</i>

18
00:01:03,782 --> 00:01:07,852
Publish/subscribe lets a producer publish an event without selecting one specific consumer.

19
00:01:08,562 --> 00:01:11,742
Independent subscribers can react to the same event, enabling fan-out.

20
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:14,140
<i>What is fan-out?</i>

21
00:01:15,654 --> 00:01:20,424
Fan-out means one event causes work in multiple independent consumers, for example analytics, notifications,

22
00:01:20,624 --> 00:01:22,564
and indexing reacting to the same domain event.

23
00:01:24,441 --> 00:01:25,821
<i>What is at-most-once delivery?</i>

24
00:01:27,074 --> 00:01:31,744
At-most-once delivery avoids broker-driven redelivery but may lose work if a message or acknowledgement fails.

25
00:01:33,241 --> 00:01:34,631
<i>What is at-least-once delivery?</i>

26
00:01:36,158 --> 00:01:39,738
At-least-once delivery retries messages, so consumers must expect duplicates.

27
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:43,528
Idempotency or deduplication is usually required for side effects.

28
00:01:45,065 --> 00:01:46,365
<i>What does exactly-once mean?</i>

29
00:01:47,886 --> 00:01:51,876
Exactly-once is meaningful only within a precisely defined boundary and set of guarantees.

30
00:01:52,586 --> 00:01:56,206
End-to-end business side effects often still require idempotency or deduplication.

31
00:01:57,729 --> 00:01:58,689
<i>What is a DLQ?</i>

32
00:02:00,195 --> 00:02:03,465
DLQ (Dead-Letter Queue) isolates messages that repeatedly fail.

33
00:02:04,175 --> 00:02:09,635
It is not a solution by itself; teams still need alerts, diagnosis, retention, correction, and safe replay procedures.

34
00:02:11,161 --> 00:02:12,481
<i>Why does ordering need a scope?</i>

35
00:02:14,011 --> 00:02:16,431
Global ordering is expensive and often unnecessary.

36
00:02:17,141 --> 00:02:21,681
Ordering per account, conversation, entity, or partition may be enough and should be stated explicitly.

37
00:02:23,201 --> 00:02:25,301
<i>How should a mobile client track asynchronous work?</i>

38
00:02:26,819 --> 00:02:29,339
The server should return a stable operation or job ID.

39
00:02:30,049 --> 00:02:32,249
A mobile process may disappear after receiving pending,

40
00:02:32,419 --> 00:02:36,249
so the client must be able to recover status later through refresh, polling, realtime updates,

41
00:02:36,419 --> 00:02:37,819
or push-triggered reconciliation.
